e end  of a person's life we're able to say  okay look enough is enough we're not  gonna like try anything too crazy you  know we're gonna let this person like  live out the rest of their natural life  and I think that's a good thing yeah I  just worry that we would never see this  happen in the United States this is a  British initiative and yeah or a UK  initiative and the truth of the matter  is we have this weird this weird kind of  concept in America of like push push  push push push until the end the idea  that people that is  did suicide is completely against the  law except we're like in Oregon I think  is one of the only states yeah maybe  there's a couple of others now where and  not even assisted suicide but what's the  word I'm looking for  that's kind of the bill that is a step  in the right direction I think that this  is like a no-brainer step in the right  direction but I bet you if we tried to  pass a law like that people would really  push back yeah so why is it that the  religious people the ones that believe  in heaven right the ones that believe in  the afterlife are also the people that  want to keep everybody on machines  they're there you know I mean they want  you to go to the netherworld and then at  the same time they want to keep you here  via science it's like again this goes  we've had a real theme with this episode  but there's this strange disconnect  between we all have in these really  emotional situations but the truth is I  think in practice more individuals would  actually you know if it was your mother  if it was your brother or sister you  would want to make the decision that  that ease their suffering but I think  that when we step away from it and we  think about it in global terms there's  this intense fear I think and I don't  know if it's coming specifically from  the right or specifically from the  religious right I should say but there's  this intense fear that like I don't want  a doctor making a decision about when I  